1
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:41,480
 Stars, galaxies, in astral seas above, on cosmic paths we'll trace, man's over wings.

2
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:57,330
 Liberty is such a wonder thing, behind us all come see, come take your place, we're leaving soon for...

3
00:01:57,330 --> 00:02:07,680
 ...nice large-scale militants on a planet to identify.

4
00:02:10,750 --> 00:02:16,930
 From what I've been able to piece together, I would say the planet is probably located within the constellation Triton.

5
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:19,520
 Our own observations would confirm this.

6
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:24,670
 We have observed an unusually heavy traffic between the constellation Triton and Cassiopeia.

7
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:29,510
 On behalf of the intergalactic government, I would like to congratulate you two for your excellent work,

8
00:02:29,890 --> 00:02:35,270
 and we would like also to express our thanks to the delegation of Auriga for its invaluable contribution to the success of this mission.

9
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,880
 Thank you again. Good work. You may both be dismissed now.

10
00:02:40,390 --> 00:02:43,650
 Gentlemen, I think it's time to put you in the picture.

11
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:47,960
 I think you should now hear the most recent message I received from Cassiopeia.

12
00:02:47,960 --> 00:03:02,740
 Now you listen here. With all the provocations from Omega and its meddling with our internal affairs, the sovereign constellation of Cassiopeia and its allies, we have taken the decision to resign from your confederation as of now.

13
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:09,640
 Only if there is adoption of a new policy recognizing our superiority, only then will we consider revising our decision.

14
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:17,880
 It is for you to decide what course you will take. Until then, any ship entering Cassiopeia's territory and that of its allies will be blasted out of the sky.

15
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:19,340
 That is my final word.

16
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:25,500
 To a declaration of war.

17
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:28,510
 I think you could say that.

18
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:30,590
 We must take immediate action.

19
00:03:30,830 --> 00:03:32,090
 We mustn't lose any time.

20
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:36,100
 As representative of a galaxy far distant from the region under consideration,

21
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,700
 may I nevertheless speak concerning this situation,

22
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:40,780
 which is, after all, of concern to Omega.

23
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:42,700
 Perhaps you could understand the situation

24
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,800
 if you saw it from the General's point of view.

25
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:46,890
 We know quite well what the General wants.

26
00:03:47,010 --> 00:03:49,050
 He seeks to use the Confederation for his own goals.

27
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:50,490
 That is obvious.

28
00:03:50,650 --> 00:03:53,430
 I have supported active campaigns in the past

29
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:54,890
 with the cooperation of Cassiopeia.

30
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:57,550
 But now I want to see such a campaign

31
00:03:57,550 --> 00:04:00,160
 against the aggressive designs of Cassiopeia.

32
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:02,080
 Madam President, what's your opinion?

33
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:03,760
 I share your opinions.

34
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:04,820
 It's a complex question,

35
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:06,600
 but there we are presented with the fact

36
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:08,660
 of what looks like an offensive armament program,

37
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:10,600
 and we must know what the target might be.

38
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:13,660
 Therefore, I shall call upon our eminent scientist philosopher

39
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:14,900
 for a likely explanation.

40
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:15,400
 Maestro?

41
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:17,760
 Oh, hi, hi, hi, Jim.

42
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:18,700
 Oh, dear, dear, dear.

43
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:19,600
 Now, let me see.

44
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:21,620
 The Triton galaxy, like, years of...

45
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:22,060
 I'm waiting.

46
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:27,280
 Madame President, gentlemen, you are aware that our own galaxy there in the Milky Way

47
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:31,040
 extends over a distance equivalent to that covered in 100 million light years.

48
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:34,900
 And you will be aware that the mean distance lying between the center of the galaxy,

49
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:39,500
 where Omega is located, and the solar system is in the region of 27,000 light years.

50
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:43,370
 Now, Triton is at this point of the triangle, whose two other points are the sun,

51
00:04:43,490 --> 00:04:46,310
 which you may see, is located here, and Omega, which is here.

52
00:04:46,410 --> 00:04:47,450
 All this is quite simple.

53
00:04:47,830 --> 00:04:50,410
 Observe that Cassiopeia is located a great distance away.

54
00:04:51,310 --> 00:04:55,010
 Centaur is very near the sun, four light years, a hop, skip, and a jump, you could say.

55
00:04:55,310 --> 00:04:59,510
 And as for Auriga, it's only 47 light years, and Alderbarra, 43.

56
00:04:59,730 --> 00:05:01,050
 Now, no distance at all, you'll agree.

57
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:06,420
 Thus, it is these planets which constitute ideal targets for any bellicose action emanating from Triton.

58
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:12,270
 What is the view of the distinguished chief of our armed forces, Colonel Pierre?

59
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:15,150
 We are in a dramatic situation of inferiority.

60
00:05:15,230 --> 00:05:19,550
 We have no ships in service able to match the heavy cruisers Cassiopeia can put up.

61
00:05:19,550 --> 00:05:23,250
 I would suggest the time has come to correct this imbalance in forces.

62
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,850
 Well, I've worked something out that might just do the trick.

63
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:28,590
 I have it here.

64
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:35,740
 Just a moment.

65
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:36,960
 Now, this gives me an idea.

66
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:38,380
 Triton, yes, Triton, of course.

67
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:39,300
 That's it, don't grab.

68
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,240
 Polar Sun.

69
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:48,420
 It's child's play at 5 billion kilometers distance.

70
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:53,760
 Unstable, it could explode into a supernova explosion at any moment.

71
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:55,160
 Not a good idea to send anyone there.

72
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,100
 Can you explain it?

73
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:57,930
 Oh, well, it's quite simple, really.

74
00:05:58,070 --> 00:05:59,370
 It's burning itself out.

75
00:05:59,370 --> 00:06:02,050
 It's a star which will run out of fuel and collapse in on itself.

76
00:06:02,430 --> 00:06:04,830
 Its outer shell is 15 times colder than its center.

77
00:06:05,050 --> 00:06:08,150
 This differential will eventually create some splendid fireworks,

78
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:12,530
 for instantly it will begin to shine as brightly as billions of sun,

79
00:06:12,650 --> 00:06:16,510
 and half of its mass will be ejected at a speed of 10,000 kilometers per second.

80
00:06:16,630 --> 00:06:17,230
 Highly destructive.

81
00:06:18,270 --> 00:06:22,450
 Yes, in the galaxy NC-34, the constellation of the Great Bear,

82
00:06:22,530 --> 00:06:26,210
 an explosion pulverized well over a million stars in a few seconds.

83
00:06:26,370 --> 00:06:29,430
 Well, we must warn the Cassiopeians immediately in case they don't know.

84
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:32,370
 Countless lives are in danger. They must be saved. Yes?

85
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:37,530
 I fear, Madam President, they will not be disposed to anyone's interfering in their plans.

86
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:40,480
 Perhaps. Nevertheless, I feel it is our duty to tell them.

87
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:43,260
 Now, may we return to the question of our spaceships in service?

88
00:06:43,650 --> 00:06:48,840
 From a strategic point of view, blowing up the whole Triton installation would do us very well.

89
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:50,740
 How can you say such a thing, Commander?

90
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:54,370
 There are humanitarian considerations. Innocent lives are at risk.

91
00:06:54,450 --> 00:06:55,510
 We haven't forgotten them.

92
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:59,630
 But how can we do anything for them when we ourselves have no adequate protection?

93
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:00,760
 Yes.

94
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:04,320
 Strategically speaking, certainly the commander is right.

95
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:05,200
 Well?

96
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,490
 All right, Colonel. You'll get your new cruisers.

97
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:10,010
 Four of the six requests.

98
00:07:10,650 --> 00:07:13,640
 And they have four times as many as that, but it's better than nothing.

99
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:15,320
 And so the problem is this.

100
00:07:15,570 --> 00:07:17,260
 Who's going to be ready first?

101
00:07:17,450 --> 00:07:19,370
 Will their base be ready to attack us,

102
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:23,370
 or will our ships be ready first and give us a chance to ward off the attacks?

103
00:07:23,890 --> 00:07:50,270
 Would be a tragedy if all these efforts came to nothing.

104
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:52,190
 What a superb new ship.

105
00:07:52,810 --> 00:07:55,780
 According to reports, their base is approaching completion.

106
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:57,860
 Faster, you workers, great galaxies.

107
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:06,150
 Let us define our spatiotemporal coordinates.

108
00:08:06,550 --> 00:08:11,150
 X, Y, Z, T with reference points R, X, Y prime, Z prime, and T prime.

109
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:15,290
 These coordinates lie in relation to reference point R prime.

110
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:17,280
 A body moving at a uniform...

111
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:18,540
 No, what's wrong?

112
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:19,600
 No, that's not right.

113
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:21,060
 Where did I get such a crazy notion?

114
00:08:21,220 --> 00:08:24,280
 Of course you realized what I meant to say.

115
00:08:25,930 --> 00:08:31,570
 A body moving in a simulated vector velocity V relative to R on the axis direction...

116
00:08:31,570 --> 00:08:36,800
 Very well, let's talk about more interesting things.

117
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:43,840
 Did you know that it was only in the 20th century that it was realized that the greater part of what we observe in the sky is located outside our galaxy?

118
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:58,100
 These observable phenomena are, in fact, independent galaxies themselves, some of them ten times the size of our Milky Way and containing billions of stars, like Andromatus, the hunting dogs, the sculptor, yes, yes, hmm.

119
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:03,550
 Now, the tiniest of these observed pinpoints of light is an infinity greater than the sun.

120
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:10,210
 which is itself quite a modestly-sized star as its explosions reach out about a million kilometers,

121
00:09:10,670 --> 00:09:15,610
 burning away as it does one day, our sun will die, having exhausted all its hydrogen fuel.

122
00:09:15,610 --> 00:09:19,070
 Of course, we would expect this. It will grow red and expand,

123
00:09:19,170 --> 00:09:22,350
 attaining the size millions and millions of times greater than its present size.

124
00:09:22,810 --> 00:09:27,650
 At that point, it will incorporate its satellites, Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, and Pluto.

125
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:32,080
 But be assured, this will not happen for another five million years.

126
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:37,140
 Here it is, our old familiar son, once more the Red Giant as we've always known it.

127
00:09:37,660 --> 00:09:39,180
 You want it, Professor?

128
00:09:39,540 --> 00:09:42,980
 Oh, now, what is it? It's such a nuisance. I shall return in a moment.

129
00:09:44,510 --> 00:09:50,680
 I have convened this session, gentlemen, to inform you all of the response to our message sent to Cassiopeia.

130
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:55,310
 It has all the tact we have come to expect from our recent ally. Mind your own business.

131
00:09:55,960 --> 00:10:00,250
 The General does not appear unduly concerned about the individuals he'd sent to their death.

132
00:10:00,570 --> 00:10:03,770
 Now the Colonel will give you a report on the study he has carried out.

133
00:10:03,770 --> 00:10:12,050
 We have observed a massive flow of traffic to the region, which implies a significant increase in activity, civilian and military, and a large buildup in material.

134
00:10:12,350 --> 00:10:16,630
 We have reason to believe that their base will soon be operational, which explains the tone of their response.

135
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:20,990
 I will now ask our expert maestro to inform us regarding the possible results of the catastrophe.

136
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:24,480
 The floor is yours, maestro, if you will go ahead and give us your opinion.

137
00:10:25,190 --> 00:10:32,290
 Well, when the gigantic explosion actually takes place throughout the universe, we will observe it for millions of light years' distance.

138
00:10:32,450 --> 00:10:37,530
 Now, in our galaxy alone, we've observed many such supernova explosions.

139
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:44,490
 In 1054, the Crab Nebula, in 1752, there was one in the Cassopoeia region, 1604, another one in the Neblia Officius.

140
00:10:44,490 --> 00:10:47,810
 In 2421, another one that was recorded in the region of Urn.

141
00:10:47,810 --> 00:10:49,130
 More to the point.

142
00:10:49,130 --> 00:10:53,130
 Well, uh, oh, yes, yes. In short, we have seen such explosions.

143
00:10:53,130 --> 00:10:59,570
 But the material that is projected out does not travel at the speed of light, which I need not remind you is 300,000 kilometers per second.

144
00:10:59,570 --> 00:11:03,730
 Hence, coming from Triton, that is a distance of five billion kilometers.

145
00:11:03,930 --> 00:11:05,530
 It would require some eight days.

146
00:11:05,630 --> 00:11:06,070
 In other words...

147
00:11:06,070 --> 00:11:08,070
 Yes, we follow you, Professor. Do go on.

148
00:11:08,230 --> 00:11:12,630
 Oh, yes. Well, in any case, all trace of life in its path would be eradicated.

149
00:11:13,490 --> 00:11:18,180
 In no case can we allow our general's madness to sacrifice a neutral colony of innocent humans.

150
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,060
 It's against the principles of our confederation.

151
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:23,440
 Now, what can we do to predict when the explosion will happen?

152
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:27,060
 Ah, well, at our distance away, alas, there's not a great deal that we can do to predict it

153
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:28,840
 because we would need to monitor the star.

154
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:30,640
 Minutes and even seconds count.

155
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:33,000
 Now, where is it? Here we go.

156
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:36,760
 Before the actual explosion, the star will undergo certain physical changes.

157
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:37,900
 A mammoth scale.

158
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:42,260
 If we were in its vicinity, it would mean using apparatus that could carry out certain tests,

159
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:48,000
 suspectographic analyses, which would most likely give us all the data that we would need to predict this explosion.

160
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:53,160
 Careful. If we send anything into the region, we would be accused of meddling in their internal affairs.

161
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:56,620
 Ah, but we can place our instruments well outside Cassiopeia's territory,

162
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:00,100
 using relay stations to carry the signal back to a control center.

163
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:01,420
 No need to go in close.

164
00:12:01,820 --> 00:12:04,560
 I have drawn this little sketch. It should clear it up.

165
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:06,540
 This is the Kohler Sun.

166
00:12:07,170 --> 00:12:10,160
 Yes, now, um, what should I say?

167
00:12:10,940 --> 00:12:13,440
 Satellite relay stations orbit the planets

168
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:17,200
 to send signals back to our space station.

169
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:21,540
 You see? A system of relays.

170
00:12:31,930 --> 00:12:41,090
 Can't they work faster? We're losing precious time.

171
00:12:41,150 --> 00:12:42,750
 Don't get worked up. It doesn't do any good.

172
00:12:42,930 --> 00:12:44,690
 Besides, they'll never be ready in time.

173
00:12:44,690 --> 00:12:48,660
 Their base is finished now, and intelligence reports indicate it's massive.

174
00:12:49,060 --> 00:12:52,640
 As near as we can tell, their countdown for attack must be underway by now.

175
00:12:52,740 --> 00:12:55,740
 When I think, here we are. We know what's going on, and we can't stop it.

176
00:12:55,800 --> 00:12:58,040
 What can we do? Attack first? With what?

177
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:01,860
 We don't even know their target. Is it Omega, Centaur, or Earth?

178
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:05,580
 If only that damn star would blow up once and for all, that'd settle it.

179
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:07,690
 Yes, that's what we need.

180
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:37,900
 So, in summary, in function of given parameters, my calculations...

181
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:40,420
 If instead of relying on your archaic mental calculations

182
00:13:40,420 --> 00:13:42,600
 you entrusted the problem to my superior ability,

183
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:43,420
 you would not regret it.

184
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:45,760
 These geniuses who would rather push buttons

185
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:47,900
 and use their gray matter, I've no time for them.

186
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:50,080
 The historic antediluvian relic you are.

187
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:51,600
 Quiet, you glorified tin can.

188
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:54,660
 Well, if my figures are right,

189
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:57,280
 the explosion of Kohler's sun ought to take place.

190
00:13:57,360 --> 00:13:57,780
 Let's see.

191
00:13:59,130 --> 00:14:00,400
 It's right now.

192
00:14:14,020 --> 00:14:34,520
 I understand.

193
00:14:35,140 --> 00:14:37,050
 Get away from there as quick as you can.

194
00:14:40,390 --> 00:14:40,830
 Observations.

195
00:14:41,010 --> 00:14:42,100
 I wish you could see it.

196
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:43,400
 It's spectacular, magnificent.

197
00:14:43,620 --> 00:14:46,160
 Madam President, a phenomenon like this is unique.

198
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:51,120
 Statistically observable at this distance, every 60 million years is an historic occasion.

199
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:53,260
 Leave the sector immediately. That is in order.

200
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:55,850
 The shockwave will hit you in less than 20 minutes.

201
00:14:56,190 --> 00:14:57,550
 Leave before it's too late, understand?

202
00:14:58,120 --> 00:15:01,290
 Transmit order to convene a General Assembly to all members of government.

203
00:15:01,650 --> 00:15:03,850
 The Council gets into emergency session. Urgent.

204
00:15:04,220 --> 00:15:07,610
 Put on General Grashley at Cassiopeia. This is a priority message.

205
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:09,170
 Hurry, it's a matter of life and death.

206
00:15:09,170 --> 00:15:12,150
 Ah, General, are you aware that the Kolaru Sun has just exploded?

207
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:16,240
 In a short time, the constellation of Triton will be hit and destroyed.

208
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:17,540
 Are you aware of it, General?

209
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:19,420
 Have you taken action to save lives?

210
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:21,950
 What? Another one of your sneaky tricks.

211
00:15:22,190 --> 00:15:24,690
 Look, our scientists are as good as yours or even better.

212
00:15:24,810 --> 00:15:27,830
 Don't worry. We'll take action if anything happens.

213
00:15:28,150 --> 00:15:30,290
 General, it's too late. We observed the explosion.

214
00:15:30,350 --> 00:15:31,810
 Stop meddling in our affairs.

215
00:15:32,510 --> 00:15:33,930
 All these planets are mine.

216
00:15:34,090 --> 00:15:37,310
 I warn you, the order is to shoot any intruding alien ship.

217
00:15:39,070 --> 00:15:41,970
 You told them all right. Well, oh, Chief, very good job.

218
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:45,000
 Put me through to the president at once. It's urgent.

219
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:46,730
 In fact, it's more than...

220
00:15:46,730 --> 00:15:48,350
 What is it, Professor? You called me?

221
00:15:48,590 --> 00:15:51,030
 Did I? Yes, I did. Of course I did. But I have an important announcement.

222
00:15:51,600 --> 00:15:56,110
 If I can trust my calculations, the explosion, or rather the supernova, will be in all probability.

223
00:15:56,230 --> 00:15:58,590
 It'll take place very soon, indeed, at this very moment.

224
00:15:59,460 --> 00:16:00,920
 At last, they're leaving.

225
00:16:01,570 --> 00:16:04,530
 Yes, my friend. Thank you. Your calculations were perfect.

226
00:16:04,930 --> 00:16:08,090
 The Monitoring Space Station has just sent us pictures of the explosion.

227
00:16:12,410 --> 00:16:19,260
 Get the same treatment. We'll shoot them down.

228
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:23,240
 We can do no more. Debris from the explosion will reach them in four hours.

229
00:16:23,380 --> 00:16:29,220
 Even if General Grouchly sent rescue ships to the base immediately, they would arrive two days after our ships.

230
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:30,660
 They would be too late.

231
00:16:31,950 --> 00:16:35,650
 Thousands of unfortunate, innocent people face a horrible, imminent death.

232
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:36,860
 What are we to do, gentlemen?

233
00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:39,920
 Red alert. Red alert.

234
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:41,420
 All crews.

235
00:16:43,150 --> 00:16:44,070
 All stations.

236
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:45,850
 General alert.

237
00:16:46,830 --> 00:16:49,600
 Mobilization of all patrol units for rescue.

238
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:37,500
 This is Captain Agar. Message received.

239
00:17:40,010 --> 00:17:42,210
 Persis. Installation.

240
00:17:55,560 --> 00:18:12,620
 Get there and...

241
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:27,010
 ...to blast any ships in our territory. Carry out those orders.

242
00:18:27,350 --> 00:18:43,620
 Destroy including ships. Fire!

243
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:47,620
 We are going into action, Captain. Standby crew at your battle stations. Here we go.

244
00:18:47,620 --> 00:19:00,680
 Message to Cassiopeia. You will stop your attack. Our mission is humanitarian.

245
00:19:00,680 --> 00:19:03,640
 If you go on with this nonsense, we will have to fire back.

246
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:22,150
 Commence firing. Aim for a vulnerable point, otherwise they're invulnerable.

247
00:19:22,150 --> 00:19:23,150
 Bloodthirsty savages...

248
00:19:23,150 --> 00:19:24,420
 Lander! Let me have him.

249
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:28,520
 I have a score to settle with that one. There are a few lessons I'd like to teach him my way.

250
00:19:28,520 --> 00:19:57,390
 Position where they can't fire on him.

251
00:19:57,390 --> 00:19:59,290
 Alright, here we go, you bunch of...

252
00:19:59,290 --> 00:20:06,080
 Torpedoes. Delayed timer. Ready, fire!

253
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:38,380
 And destroy every OMA-

254
00:20:38,380 --> 00:20:42,540
 Military or civilian, anywhere in the region of our base on Triton.

255
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:44,060
 Now carry out your order.

256
00:20:44,060 --> 00:20:52,340
 General, we've just received word from Triton that Kohler's son has just exploded, General.

257
00:20:52,340 --> 00:20:56,930
 See?

258
00:20:56,930 --> 00:21:02,900
 Change in your orders. Now you are to send all craft directly to our Triton installation.

259
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:06,790
 Save the equipment, understand? The equipment first.

260
00:21:06,790 --> 00:21:24,150
 The men-

261
00:21:24,150 --> 00:22:03,930
 GGs.

262
00:22:03,930 --> 00:22:18,160
 And prevent-

263
00:22:18,160 --> 00:22:19,450
 Who haven't left.

264
00:22:19,450 --> 00:22:21,250
 There's no time to lose.

265
00:22:21,250 --> 00:22:22,050
 We're late.

266
00:22:22,050 --> 00:22:23,610
 Every second counts.

267
00:22:32,400 --> 00:22:42,310
 See, everybody will be safe.

268
00:22:43,030 --> 00:22:45,150
 Back in the city there, you sure there's nobody left?

269
00:22:45,690 --> 00:22:46,630
 They say there's some.

270
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:50,050
 It's now or never, young fellow.

271
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:51,920
 But I think they're not all here.

272
00:22:52,190 --> 00:22:54,290
 Well, go ahead, don't lose any time.

273
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:03,510
 But he's still in the city.

274
00:23:06,310 --> 00:23:09,590
 The last seaship will leave in a few minutes.

275
00:23:38,410 --> 00:24:07,800
 After one second more and...

276
00:24:07,800 --> 00:25:40,640
 Since anchors away, auxiliary power unit is a planet too far away.

277
00:25:40,720 --> 00:25:44,030
 I got a hunch on this old lady, quick as we can.

278
00:25:47,640 --> 00:25:48,790
 Notify Omega.

279
00:25:49,350 --> 00:25:50,960
 I do, but the radio's dead.

280
00:25:50,960 --> 00:25:51,810
 We'll try to fix it.

